diff --git a/tests/p5/BXCAN.md b/tests/p5/BXCAN.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..65fef9e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/p5/BXCAN.md
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# P5 bxCAN acceptance scope
+
+The production F4 backend is derived from the last official F4 source,
+`commaai/panda@3dc21386239e3073a623156b75901aa302340d6c`, and uses the locked
+current opendbc packet and safety ABI. Historical safety code is not restored.
+The current 72-byte aligned packet and six-byte wire header remain unchanged;
+F4 accepts classic CAN DLC 0–8 only. Host ingress rejects FD, oversized DLC,
+invalid buses/flags/standard IDs and checksum errors before a safety hook runs.
+The mailbox path repeats validation before writing controller registers.
+
+`test_bxcan.c` directly includes the production `bxcan.h`, `can_common.h` and
+locked `opendbc/safety/safety.h`. Its explicit mocks cover only low-level CAN
+register effects, IRQ registration and LEDs. Tests invoke the actual noOutput
+and allOutput hooks; allOutput is a host fixture for inspecting mailbox and
+forwarding behavior, not authorization to transmit on a vehicle.
+
+`test_llbxcan.c` directly includes the full production `llbxcan.h`. Register
+writes and CAN acknowledgement state are simulated to exercise timing register
+values, bounded initialization failures, filter ownership, IRQ wiring and exact
+write-one-to-clear masks. CAN1 and CAN2 share CAN1's filter registers with the
+split fixed at bank 14; CAN3 has independent filters. These tests cannot establish
+clock accuracy, electrical CAN operation, bus recovery timing or board pinout.
+
+Run `run_bxcan.py --source <panda-root> --opendbc <opendbc-root> --output <dir>`.
+The runner compiles with strict warnings and AddressSanitizer/UBSan, saves the
+compiler input hashes and requires every named group to run exactly once.
+Mac AddressSanitizer does not support leak detection; the tests use no heap
+allocation. The Linux run additionally enables leak detection. Actual STM32
+compilation and H7 protocol/safety regression are separate acceptance steps.
+
+
+`run_fdcan.py` separately exercises the actual current H7 `fdcan.h` with the
+same actual queues and safety hooks. Host address registers cannot model a
+32-bit MCU address space directly, so its generated include makes exactly three
+verified HAL substitutions: widen the TX/RX message-RAM address temporaries to
+`uintptr_t`, and replace the RX FIFO acknowledgement store with a simulated
+acknowledgement. Full original/adapted source hashes and the replacements are
+recorded. All CAN policy, FD/BRS fields, payload handling, forwarding, error and
+queue code remains the actual source. Fake message RAM is a static array; no
+mapping, device descriptor or hardware address is accessed. Queue bank placement
+is checked separately in the ARM ELF audit. This is not MCU SPI or USB electrical
+validation.
diff --git a/tests/p5/README.md b/tests/p5/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b5945e0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/p5/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,75 @@
+# P5 offline protocol, ABI, safety and resource workers
+
+These workers do not import Panda, enumerate USB, open serial devices, reset,
+flash, or run on a microcontroller. Run them inside the engineering project's
+network/device-denied validation wrapper. Dependencies must already exist.
+
+## Interfaces
+
+* `run_validation.py --panda TREE --opendbc TREE --output DIR --arm-prefix BIN`
+  compiles `abi_probe.c` with the locked `arm-none-eabi-gcc` for Cortex-M4 and
+  Cortex-M7. It extracts target object sections with `arm-none-eabi-objcopy`;
+  the host compiler does not establish the target ABI. Both targets must retain
+  health 48 bytes, CAN-health 64, CANPacket 72/alignment 4/data offset 6, the
+  complete 64-byte payload storage, exact pinned header hashes and a bitfield
+  wire golden. Each target must reject three old ABI compile expectations.
+  The native protocol suites use ASan/UBSan and real `can_common.h`,
+  `can_comms.h`, and opendbc safety hooks. Complete selected `main_comms.h`
+  branches and `get_health_pkt` are extracted without rewriting their bodies;
+  exact source and generated hashes are reported. HAL observations, CAN process
+  callbacks and interrupt locking are fixture stubs. The H7 policy suite tests
+  the non-F4 policy on the host; it does not emulate an H7 controller.
+* `run_safety.py --opendbc ARCHIVE --output DIR` compiles the current actual
+  `libsafety` C twice, with and without `ALLOW_DEBUG`. Use a source archive
+  without `.git` because upstream's compiler writes temporary objects next to
+  `safety.c`. The concrete noOutput, silent, framework and BYD classes are
+  selected, plus explicit bus/length, expiry-boundary and release-policy checks.
+  Five inapplicable/empty BYD methods are explicitly listed as unselected, not
+  counted as passes. BYD safety 35 is the official debug-only Atto3 reference;
+  these tests provide no Song PLUS actuation or vehicle-safety approval.
+* `run_firmware_liveness.py --panda TREE --opendbc TREE --target f4|h7
+  --output DIR --compiler clang` carries forward the P4 source oracle and
+  freshly extracts the candidate's actual tick, safety-mode, fault and software
+  watchdog functions and heartbeat control branches. Its 13 cases run in
+  separate processes. HAL and safety-hook calls are explicit intent stubs;
+  actual safety-hook behavior belongs to the separate safety suite. Modified
+  P5 files are measured here and pinned by the outer patch manifest; unchanged
+  files retain the P4 SHA pins. Heartbeat counters are tick counts, not precise
+  wall-clock deadlines. A stopped software-watchdog loop cannot act until it
+  resumes; late-loop fault recording itself is distinct from entering silent.
+* `audit_resources.py --target f4|h7 --elf FILE --map FILE --raw FILE
+  --signed FILE --public-key FILE --stack-dir DIR --output FILE` reads the
+  actual linked ELF32, map, raw/signed application and fresh compiler `.su`/
+  `.ci` outputs. It checks the app origin, no load into the boot region, all
+  RAM sections, static queues, F4 stack/cookie exclusion, image/version/signature
+  tail, debug RSA signature against the public key, and target DMA placement.
+  H7's preserved SPI DMA uses byte transfers and needs byte alignment, not
+  word alignment. F4 SPI buffers must be absent. F4 USB uses CPU FIFO accesses.
+
+## Deliberate limits
+
+The resource audit calculates individual frames and the known direct call
+graph, and lists unresolved/indirect callees. Neither this nor a successful
+link proves worst-case nested interrupt/indirect-call stack usage. The reserved
+stack is a compile-time budget; a runtime high-water measurement remains open.
+
+The preserved H7 baseline has an inherited overlay: its four-byte
+`enter_bootloader_mode` at `0x38001ffc` lies inside `mic_tx_buf`
+`[0x38001770, 0x38002770)`. Its `fault_cookie` at `0x38003ffc` has no overlapping
+object. The audit calculates both cookies' object overlaps from each actual
+ELF and requires precisely this existing H7 layout; it reports
+`isolated_from_live_objects: false` for H7. This is preservation evidence, not
+a claim that the overlay's runtime or reset behavior is safe. F4 requires no
+live-object overlap for either cookie and reserves their dedicated section.
+
+The F4 capability response is separate from the unchanged health/0xdd ABI.
+Its unavailable mask marks sound, SPI-error and current readings as absent.
+Canonical zero fields are therefore not measurements. Temperature-provider
+failure invalidates the entire health response rather than fabricating a
+temperature. These protocol fixtures validate serialization and error behavior;
+the real ADC/pin providers have their own register-level BSP tests.
+
+An application signed with the repository's development key is an offline
+candidate, including a candidate compiled without debug safety modes. No
+installed bootstub/signature acceptance or unknown C3XL wiring is established.
+No bootstub binary or recovery procedure is produced by these workers.
